{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Mild positive bias at short horizons (≈D+1 to D+10): model is typically ~0.1–0.6 €/t too low versus realized; apply a small upward offset early in the path.",
    "Strong negative bias at longer horizons (≈D+18 to D+30): model systematically undershoots (often by ~1–6 €/t) and sometimes predicts a drop that does not materialize; dampen late-horizon selloffs and pull forecasts back up toward the prevailing level.",
    "Overreaction/overshoot to perceived mean reversion after local peaks: when recent history rises into the mid/high-60s, the model often forecasts a sharp decline into low-60s that is not realized; cap the magnitude of forecasted drawdowns following an upswing (reduce negative slope).",
    "Insufficient persistence in uptrends: realized paths stay elevated longer than forecast; increase trend persistence (higher weight on last 5–10 days) especially beyond ~2 weeks.",
    "Horizon-dependent shrinkage: apply stronger mean-reversion toward the recent 10–15d average for mid-horizons (≈D+10 to D+20), but reduce mean-reversion for far horizons (≈D+20 to D+30) where the model currently reverts too hard downward."
  ]
}
